\title{The Thermodynamics of Proof Normalization:\\
Fiber Entropy, Bureaucratic Calculi, and Compositional Landauer Accounting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Proof normalization is a many-to-one operation, and by Landauer's principle any physical
realization of a many-to-one map must dissipate heat proportional to the information it
destroys. This paper develops an exact information-theoretic accounting for that dissipation
on finite proof systems and establishes four principal results. (i) The \emph{Fiber-Entropy
Law}: for a finite normalization map $f$ and an arbitrary non-negative weight $p$ on proof
terms, the Shannon entropy destroyed, $H_p(x \mid f x)$, is bounded above by the expected
logarithm of the normalization-fiber size, $\mathbb{E}_p[\log_2 |f^{-1}(f x)|]$, with
equality \emph{if and only if} $p$ is constant on each fiber; any fiberwise non-uniformity
makes the bound strict. (ii) An explicit family of \emph{strongly normalizing calculi with
exponential fibers and short conclusions}: for each $n$ a rewrite system with $n$ rules,
$n$-bit conclusions, well-founded reduction, characterized and unique normal forms,
normalization in at most $n$ steps, and normalization fibers of cardinality exactly $2^n$,
destroying exactly $n$ bits and dissipating exactly $n k_B T \ln 2$ of heat. (iii)
\emph{Compositional Landauer accounting}: the difference between separate and joint
verification cost equals the \emph{drop} in mutual information, $I(\mathrm{in}) -
I(\mathrm{out})$; the naive form ``saving $=$ mutual information'' is false in general and
correct exactly when verification destroys all correlation. (iv) A \emph{pipeline dichotomy}:
conditional entropy is exactly additive along composite normalizations, while the
fiber-counting estimate is only subadditive for uniform laws, with an exact equality
criterion, and fails to be subadditive at all for skewed laws. Entropy composes; multiplicity
does not.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Erasure as the only thermodynamic cost of computation}

Landauer's principle states that erasing one bit of information in a physical device at
temperature $T$ dissipates at least $k_B T \ln 2$ of heat. Its converse --- that logically
reversible computation has no unavoidable thermodynamic cost --- makes the principle a
statement about \emph{information destruction} specifically. Whenever a computational process
implements a non-injective map, the informational deficit lower-bounds the entropy exported
to the environment.

Proof normalization is such a process. Cut elimination, $\beta$-normal form computation,
permutative (commuting) conversions, and every other proof-tidying operation take many
derivations to a single canonical one. The preimages of a canonical proof under
normalization --- its \emph{normalization fiber} --- record precisely the bookkeeping that
the normalizer throws away.

\subsection{The counting heuristic and its failure}

The first estimate one writes down for the cost of a normalization step is the logarithm of
the fiber size; averaged over the proof law $p$ this gives the purely combinatorial
\emph{fiber-counting estimate} $\mathbb{E}_p[\log_2 |f^{-1}(f x)|]$. The thermodynamically
correct quantity is the conditional entropy $H_p(x \mid f x)$, which is statistical. They
coincide only under a condition, and the first business of this paper is to identify that
condition exactly and to show that outside it the counting estimate is \emph{strictly}
pessimistic.

\section{Setting and basic functionals}

Throughout, $X$ and $Y$ are finite non-empty sets --- a set of proof terms and a set of
normal proofs or verification outcomes --- and $f : X \to Y$ is the normalization or
verification map. All logarithms written $\log_2$ are to base $2$ and all entropies are
measured in bits. A \emph{law} on $X$ is a function $p : X \to \mathbb{R}$ with $p \ge 0$; we
do \emph{not} assume $\sum_x p(x) = 1$ unless stated, since several results are needed for
unnormalized multiplicity counts.

\begin{definition}[Fiber]
For $b \in Y$, the fiber of $f$ over $b$ is $f^{-1}(b) = \{x \in X : f(x) = b\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pushforward]
$(f_*p)(b) = \sum_{x \in f^{-1}(b)} p(x)$ for $b \in Y$. It is a law of the same total mass.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Entropy]
The unnormalized Shannon entropy of a law $q$ on a finite set $S$ is
$H(q) = -\sum_{s \in S} q(s)\log_2 q(s)$, with $0\log_2 0 = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Entropy destroyed by $f$]
\[
H_p(x \mid f x) \;=\; -\sum_{x \in X} p(x)\,\log_2 \frac{p(x)}{(f_*p)(f x)} .
\]
For a probability law this is the usual conditional entropy of $x$ given $f(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Expected fiber logarithm]
$L_p(f) = \sum_{x \in X} p(x)\, \log_2 |f^{-1}(f x)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Landauer cost]
$\Land(B) = B \cdot k_B T \ln 2$. Since $k_B T \ln 2 > 0$, every identity or inequality
between bit counts transfers verbatim to energies.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform law]
$u_X(x) = 1/|X|$ for all $x \in X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Fiberwise regrouping]\label{lem:regroup}
For any $h : Y \to \mathbb{R}$, $\sum_{x} p(x)h(f x) = \sum_{b} (f_*p)(b) h(b)$. In
particular
\[
L_p(f) = \sum_{b \in Y} (f_*p)(b)\,\log_2 |f^{-1}(b)|,\qquad
H_p(x \mid f x) = \sum_{b\in Y}\Big(-\!\!\sum_{x \in f^{-1}(b)}\!\! p(x)\log_2 \tfrac{p(x)}{(f_*p)(b)}\Big).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $X$ into the fibers of $f$; the function $h(f x)$ is constant on each.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Chain rule]\label{lem:chain}
For every non-negative law $p$, $H_p(x \mid f x) = H(p) - H(f_*p)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\log_2(p(x)/(f_*p)(f x))$ as a difference of logarithms and apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:regroup} to the second term.
\end{proof}

\section{The Fiber-Entropy Law}

\subsection{A sharp Gibbs estimate for unnormalized weights}

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise Gibbs estimate]\label{lem:gibbs}
For $t \ge 0$ and $c > 0$ we have $t(\ln c - \ln t) \le c - t$, with equality if and only if
$t = c$. At $t = 0$ the inequality is strict.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $t > 0$ divide by $t$ and set $r = c/t$: the claim becomes $\ln r \le r - 1$, whose
equality case is $r = 1$. The case $t = 0$ is immediate since $c > 0$.
\end{proof}

The strictness at $t = 0$ is not a technicality: a proof term of weight zero inside a fiber of
positive mass is already a violation of fiberwise uniformity, and the law below must, and
does, report it as one.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp maximum entropy for unnormalized weights]\label{thm:maxent}
Let $S$ be finite, $q : S \to \mathbb{R}$ with $q \ge 0$ on $S$, and $Q = \sum_{s\in S} q(s)$.
Then
\[
-\sum_{s \in S} q(s)\log_2\frac{q(s)}{Q} \;\le\; Q \log_2 |S|,
\]
with equality \emph{iff} $q(s) = Q/|S|$ for all $s \in S$. No normalization, positivity, or
non-emptiness hypothesis is required; $S = \emptyset$ and $q \equiv 0$ are genuine degenerate
instances.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $Q = 0$ then $q \equiv 0$ on $S$ and both sides vanish. Otherwise put $c = Q/|S| > 0$ and
apply Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbs} termwise with $t = q(s)$:
$\sum_s q(s)(\ln c - \ln q(s)) \le \sum_s (c - q(s)) = |S|c - Q = 0$. Rewriting the left side
via $\ln c = \ln Q - \ln|S|$ and dividing by $\ln 2$ yields the inequality. Since all termwise
inequalities point the same way, the sums agree exactly when every term does, i.e.\ when
$q(s) = c$ for all $s$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The law}

\begin{theorem}[Fiber-Entropy Law]\label{thm:law}
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a normalization map on finite sets and $p$ any non-negative law on $X$.
Then $H_p(x \mid f x) \le L_p(f)$, and
\[
H_p(x \mid f x) = L_p(f) \iff \forall x,y \in X:\ f(x) = f(y) \Rightarrow p(x) = p(y).
\]
The entropy destroyed by normalization equals the expected logarithm of the
normalization-fiber size precisely when the conditional law is uniform on every fiber.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:regroup} both sides decompose as sums over $b \in Y$ of the two sides of
Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent} applied to $S = f^{-1}(b)$, $q = p|_S$, $Q = (f_*p)(b)$. Summing
gives the bound. A sum of termwise-dominated terms equals its dominating sum exactly when
each term does, so equality holds iff $p$ is constant on every fiber, which is the stated
criterion.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict form]
If $f(x) = f(y)$ and $p(x) \ne p(y)$ for some $x,y$, then $H_p(x \mid f x) < L_p(f)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Landauer form]
For $k_B, T > 0$, $\Land(H_p(x \mid fx)) = \Land(L_p(f))$ iff $p$ is fiberwise constant, and
otherwise the left side is strictly smaller. The fiber-counting heuristic never under-charges
a single normalization step and over-charges strictly off the fiberwise-uniform locus.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The uniform law is always exact]\label{cor:unifexact}
For $p = u_X$ the criterion holds trivially, so $H_{u_X}(x \mid f x) = L_{u_X}(f)$ for every
$f$. Under a uniform law the fiber count and the destroyed entropy are literally the same
number.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{A worked strict instance}

\begin{example}
Let $X = \{x_0,x_1,x_2\}$, $Y = \{y_0,y_1\}$, $f(x_0) = f(x_1) = y_0$, $f(x_2) = y_1$, and
$p = (\tfrac12,\tfrac14,\tfrac14)$. The fibers have sizes $2$ and $1$, so
$L_p(f) = \tfrac12 + \tfrac14 = \tfrac34$, while $f_*p = (\tfrac34,\tfrac14)$ gives
\[
H_p(x \mid f x) = -\tfrac12\log_2\tfrac23 - \tfrac14\log_2\tfrac13
= \tfrac34\log_2 3 - \tfrac12 \approx 0.68872 .
\]
The gap is exactly $\tfrac54 - \tfrac34\log_2 3 \approx 0.06128$ bits, strictly positive
because $p$ is not constant on the fiber $\{x_0,x_1\}$.
\end{example}

\section{A strongly normalizing calculus with exponential fibers and short conclusions}

\subsection{The bureaucratic calculus}

\begin{definition}
Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$. A \emph{derivation} of the calculus $\Bur_n$ is a pair
$d = (u,c) \in \{0,1\}^n \times \{0,1\}^n$, where $c$ is the \emph{conclusion}, an $n$-bit
statement, and $u$ is the \emph{bookkeeping vector}: $u_i = 1$ records that the $i$-th of $n$
independent inference blocks was carried out in the bureaucratic (permuted) order rather than
the canonical one.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rewrite relation]
$\Bur_n$ has exactly $n$ rules. Rule $i$ applies to $d = (u,c)$ when $u_i = 1$ and yields
$(u[i \mapsto 0], c)$. We write $d \to e$ for a single rule application and $\to^*$ for its
reflexive-transitive closure.
\end{definition}

The rules change no conclusion: they are permutative conversions, the archetype of pure proof
bureaucracy.

\begin{definition}
$d = (u,c)$ is \emph{normal} if $u_i = 0$ for all $i$. The \emph{normalization map} is
$\nf(u,c) = (\mathbf{0},c)$, and the \emph{weight} of $d$ is $w(d) = |\{i : u_i = 1\}|$.
Always $w(d) \le n$, and $w(d) = 0$ iff $d$ is normal.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Strong normalization}

\begin{lemma}[Weight decrease]\label{lem:wdec}
If $d \to e$ then $w(e) = w(d) - 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rule $i$ requires $u_i = 1$, so $i$ lies in the support $\{j : u_j = 1\}$, and the support of
the result is that set with $i$ removed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strong normalization]\label{thm:sn}
The relation $\to$ is well-founded: no derivation admits an infinite reduction sequence under
any strategy.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:wdec}, $\to$ is a sub-relation of the inverse image of $<$ on $\mathbb{N}$
along $w$. Inverse images of well-founded relations are well-founded, and sub-relations of
well-founded relations are well-founded.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Normal forms: characterized and unique}

\begin{theorem}[Characterization of normal forms]
$d$ is irreducible if and only if $d$ is normal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If every $u_i = 0$ no rule applies; if some $u_i = 1$ then rule $i$ applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Conclusions are invariant]\label{lem:concl}
If $d \to^* e$ then $e$ and $d$ have the same conclusion.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Unique normal forms]
If $d \to^* e$ and $e$ is normal then $e = \nf(d)$. In particular $\Bur_n$ is confluent and
normalization is strategy-independent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Normality forces the bookkeeping component of $e$ to be $\mathbf{0}$ and
Lemma~\ref{lem:concl} forces its conclusion to be that of $d$; together these determine $e$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Normalization is linear-time}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded-length normalization]\label{thm:steps}
For every $d$, the normal form $\nf(d)$ is reachable from $d$ in exactly $w(d) \le n$ steps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $w(d)$. If $w(d) = 0$ then $d = \nf(d)$. Otherwise pick $i$ with
$u_i = 1$, apply rule $i$ to obtain $d'$ with $w(d') = w(d)-1$ and $\nf(d') = \nf(d)$, and use
the induction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Exponential fibers}

\begin{lemma}[Fiber description]\label{lem:fibdesc}
For every $d = (u,c)$, $\nf^{-1}(\nf(d)) = \{0,1\}^n \times \{c\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential fibers with short conclusions]\label{thm:expfib}
For every derivation $d$, $|\nf^{-1}(\nf(d))| = 2^n$, and each of these $2^n$ preimages
reaches $\nf(d)$ in at most $n$ rewrite steps, while the conclusion and its normal proof are
only $n$ bits long.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:fibdesc} and $|\{0,1\}^n| = 2^n$; the reachability claim is
Theorem~\ref{thm:steps} applied to each fiber member.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Proof-term multiplicity is controlled by no syntactic size parameter: the theorem length, the
canonical proof length and the normalization time are all $n$, while the multiplicity is
$2^n$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Exact thermodynamic cost}

$\Bur_n$ has $2^n \cdot 2^n = 4^n$ derivations, and the uniform law on derivations is
trivially fiberwise uniform, so Theorem~\ref{thm:law} applies in its equality case.

\begin{theorem}[Exact bit count]
Under the uniform law on derivations, $L_u(\nf) = H_u(d \mid \nf(d)) = n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every fiber has size $2^n$, so each of the $4^n$ summands of $L_u$ contributes
$4^{-n}\log_2 2^n$. The second equality is Corollary~\ref{cor:unifexact}.
\end{proof}

Writing $\erased(f) = \log_2|X| - \log_2|f(X)|$ for the image-counting erasure, the image of
$\nf$ is in bijection with the set of conclusions, so
$\erased(\nf) = \log_2 4^n - \log_2 2^n = n$ as well.

\begin{corollary}[Exact Landauer heat]
Normalizing $\Bur_n$ dissipates exactly $n\, k_B T \ln 2$ of heat --- an identity, not a
bound, precisely because the uniform law is fiberwise uniform.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Packaged statement]
For every $n$, the calculus $\Bur_n$ is finitely presented ($n$ rules), strongly normalizing,
has $n$-bit conclusions, normalizes in at most $n$ steps, has normalization fibers of
cardinality exactly $2^n$, and destroys exactly $n$ bits under the uniform law.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[$n = 3$]
Eight conclusions; $64$ derivations; eight normal derivations; every fiber of size $8$;
$\log_2 8 = 3$ bits erased; at most three rewrite steps from any derivation.
\end{example}

\section{Compositional accounting: what sharing a lemma saves}

Let two proof obligations be verified by $f : X_1 \to Y_1$ and $g : X_2 \to Y_2$, and let $p$
be a non-negative law on $X_1 \times X_2$ with marginals $p_1(x) = \sum_y p(x,y)$ and
$p_2(y) = \sum_x p(x,y)$. Write $I(p) = H(p_1) + H(p_2) - H(p)$ for the mutual information,
$f \times g$ for the product map, and $P = (f\times g)_*p$.

\begin{lemma}[Verification commutes with marginalization]\label{lem:marg}
$\big((f\times g)_*p\big)_1 = f_*(p_1)$ and $\big((f\times g)_*p\big)_2 = g_*(p_2)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The fiber of $f \times g$ over $(b_1,b_2)$ is $f^{-1}(b_1) \times g^{-1}(b_2)$; both sides
expand to the same double sum, identified by interchanging the order of summation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Compositional Landauer accounting]\label{thm:comp}
For every non-negative joint law $p$,
\[
\Big[H_{p_1}(x \mid f x) + H_{p_2}(y \mid g y)\Big] - H_p\big((x,y)\mid(fx,gy)\big)
\;=\; I(p) - I\big((f\times g)_*p\big).
\]
The saving realized by verifying two obligations jointly rather than separately is exactly the
mutual information that verification destroys.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:chain} three times --- to $f$ under $p_1$, to $g$ under $p_2$, and to
$f\times g$ under $p$ --- and regroup:
$[H(p_1)+H(p_2)-H(p)] - [H(f_*p_1)+H(g_*p_2)-H(P)]$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:marg} the second
bracket is $I(P)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The naive conjecture is false]
The form ``saving $=$ mutual information'' asserts $\text{saving} = I(p)$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} shows this holds \emph{iff} $I(P) = 0$, i.e.\ iff the verifiers destroy
all correlation. The invariant is the mutual-information \emph{drop}.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity for independent obligations]
If $p = p_1 \otimes p_2$ is a product of probability laws, then
$H_p((x,y)\mid(fx,gy)) = H_{p_1}(x \mid fx) + H_{p_2}(y \mid gy)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For a product law $I(p) = 0$, and the pushforward of a product is the product of the
pushforwards, so $I(P) = 0$ as well; Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} forces the saving to vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Data-processing inequality for mutual information]\label{thm:dpi}
For a strictly positive joint law $p$, $I\big((f\times g)_*p\big) \le I(p)$: deterministic
verification cannot manufacture statistical dependence between obligations.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $m(x,y) = p_1(x)p_2(y)$ one has $I(p) = \KL(p \Vert m)$, an identity valid with no
normalization hypothesis and with zero weights allowed. Relative entropy is monotone under
pushforward along a deterministic map, by the log-sum inequality
\[
\Big(\sum_i a_i\Big)\log_2\frac{\sum_i a_i}{\sum_i b_i} \le \sum_i a_i \log_2\frac{a_i}{b_i}
\qquad (a_i \ge 0,\ b_i > 0),
\]
itself a consequence of Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbs}, applied fiberwise and summed. Finally
$(f\times g)_*m$ is the product of the pushed marginals by Lemma~\ref{lem:marg}, so the pushed
divergence is exactly $I(P)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharing never costs]
For a strictly positive joint law,
$H_p((x,y)\mid(fx,gy)) \le H_{p_1}(x \mid fx) + H_{p_2}(y \mid gy)$.
\end{corollary}

The positivity hypothesis is genuinely needed: a joint law assigning weight zero to some pair
makes the product reference law degenerate. Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} itself needs only $p \ge 0$.

\begin{example}[The shared-lemma ledger]
Let $X_1 = X_2 = \{0,1\}$ with $p(0,0) = p(1,1) = \tfrac12$ and $p(0,1) = p(1,0) = 0$ ---
``the same lemma twice'' --- and let both verifiers be the total collapse to a one-element
set. Then $p_1 = p_2 = (\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$, $H(p_1) = H(p_2) = H(p) = 1$, so $I(p) = 1$,
while $I(P) = 0$. Separate verification destroys $1 + 1 = 2$ bits, joint verification
destroys $1$ bit, and the saving $2 - 1 = 1 = I(p) - I(P)$ is realized exactly.
\end{example}

\section{Pipelines: entropy composes, multiplicity does not}

Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$, so that $g \circ f$ is a two-stage pipeline.

\begin{lemma}[Pushforwards compose]\label{lem:pushcomp}
$(g\circ f)_*p = g_*(f_*p)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Pipeline chain rule]\label{thm:pipechain}
For every non-negative law $p$,
\[
H_p\big(x \mid g(f(x))\big) = H_p\big(x \mid f(x)\big) + H_{f_*p}\big(y \mid g(y)\big),
\]
with no correction term, at any law.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:chain} to $g\circ f$ under $p$, to $f$ under $p$, and to $g$ under
$f_*p$; the right-hand sides telescope, using Lemma~\ref{lem:pushcomp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Subadditivity under a uniform law]\label{thm:subadd}
For $p = u_X$, $\;L_{u_X}(g\circ f) \le L_{u_X}(f) + L_{f_*u_X}(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Under $u_X$ both $f$ and $g \circ f$ attain the equality case of Theorem~\ref{thm:law}, so
their fiber counts are their conditional entropies; the second stage is only bounded. Combine
with Theorem~\ref{thm:pipechain}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact equality criterion]
Equality holds in Theorem~\ref{thm:subadd} iff all $f$-fibers lying over a common $g$-fiber
have the same cardinality:
\[
L_{u_X}(g\circ f) = L_{u_X}(f) + L_{f_*u_X}(g)
\iff \forall b,b' \in Y:\ g(b) = g(b') \Rightarrow |f^{-1}(b)| = |f^{-1}(b')|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equality holds iff the second stage attains its bound, i.e.\ iff $f_*u_X$ is constant on each
$g$-fiber by Theorem~\ref{thm:law}. Since $(f_*u_X)(b) = |f^{-1}(b)|/|X|$, this is exactly
equality of the corresponding $f$-fiber sizes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of subadditivity for skewed laws]\label{thm:notsub}
There is a pipeline of finite sets and a fully supported law with
$L_p(f) + L_{f_*p}(g) < L_p(g \circ f)$: the two-stage fiber count strictly under-reports the
honest one-stage count.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch, explicit counterexample]
Take $X = \{x_0,x_1,x_2\}$, $Y = \{y_0,y_1\}$, $Z$ a point, $f(x_0) = y_0$,
$f(x_1) = f(x_2) = y_1$, $g$ the total collapse, and $p = (\tfrac45,\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{10})$.
Then $L_p(f) = \tfrac15$, $f_*p = (\tfrac45,\tfrac15)$ and $L_{f_*p}(g) = 1$, for a two-stage
total of $\tfrac65 = 1.2$; but $g\circ f$ has the single fiber $X$ of size $3$, so
$L_p(g\circ f) = \log_2 3 \approx 1.58496$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pipeline dichotomy]
On the very pipeline and law of Theorem~\ref{thm:notsub}, the entropy accounting is exactly
additive while the fiber-counting accounting is not even subadditive.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Why it fails]
The composite fiber size is the sum $\sum_{b \in g^{-1}(c)} |f^{-1}(b)|$, an $\ell^1$
quantity, while the two-stage estimate is a weighted geometric mean of the same fiber sizes
plus $\log_2$ of the $g$-fiber size. Under a uniform law the weights are proportional to the
fiber sizes and the two effects cancel; under a law concentrating on a small fiber next to a
large one they do not.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms and applications}

\subsection{Computing the accounting}

All functionals above are computable in time linear in $|X|$ once the fibers of $f$ are known.
A single pass over $X$ evaluating $f$ and accumulating counts and masses keyed by $Y$ yields
both $|f^{-1}(b)|$ and $(f_*p)(b)$ in $O(|X|)$ time and $O(|Y|)$ space. Given this census,
$L_p(f)$ and $H_p(x \mid fx)$ each need one further pass, and the fiberwise-uniformity test a
third; Theorem~\ref{thm:law} then predicts, before the numbers are compared, whether the gap
will be zero or strictly positive --- a cheap consistency check on any implementation.
Reporting the per-fiber defects $\log_2|f^{-1}(b)| - H(q_b)$, with $q_b$ the conditional law
inside fiber $b$, localizes the over-charge to the fibers responsible for it.

\subsection{Guidance for proof pipelines}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Do not size an energy budget by counting derivations.} For a single step the
count is an upper bound, tight only when all derivations of a theorem are equally likely.
\item \textbf{Do not compose fiber counts.} The composite estimate can strictly exceed the
sum of the stagewise estimates, so stagewise counting is not even conservative. Compose
entropies instead: Theorem~\ref{thm:pipechain} is exact and unconditional.
\item \textbf{Share lemmas, and measure the sharing correctly.} Sharing never hurts, and the
benefit is the correlation destroyed, strictly less than the correlation present whenever the
outputs remain correlated.
\item \textbf{Expect multiplicity blowup even for small theorems.} $n$-bit theorems with
$n$-bit canonical proofs normalizing in $n$ steps can have $2^n$ derivations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Reading the numbers physically}

At room temperature $k_B T \ln 2 \approx 2.87 \times 10^{-21}\,\mathrm{J}$ per bit. The
bureaucratic calculus at $n = 40$ thus has fibers of about $10^{12}$ derivations and a
normalization cost of about $1.15 \times 10^{-19}\,\mathrm{J}$ per proof: negligible in
absolute terms, which is precisely why the interest lies in the identities rather than the
joules. Landauer accounting is used here as an invariant constraining proof transformations,
not as an engineering constraint.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

\textbf{Finiteness.} Everything is stated for finite sets of proof terms, the natural setting
for exact fiber cardinalities and a well-defined Shannon entropy. An asymptotic theory over
unbounded proof size would require care with the choice of universal coding.

\textbf{Presentation of the calculus.} $\Bur_n$ is finitely presented for each $n$, with $n$
rules. It is a family, not a single infinite calculus, and no claim is made that it is one.
Its rules are permutative conversions on independent blocks: a deliberately minimal model of
proof bureaucracy rather than full cut elimination.

\textbf{Strength of the normalization claim.} Theorem~\ref{thm:sn} asserts well-foundedness of
the rewrite relation, hence termination of every strategy, not merely the existence of a
terminating strategy; normal forms are characterized and identified rather than postulated.

\textbf{Hypotheses of the compositional results.} The accounting identity needs only
$p \ge 0$; non-negativity of the saving genuinely needs strict positivity, and the boundary is
stated explicitly.

\textbf{Scope of the counterexample.} Theorem~\ref{thm:notsub} uses a fully supported law, so
the failure of subadditivity is not an artefact of point masses; and the uniform-law equality
criterion is an ``iff'', so the boundary between the regimes is exact.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{A. The fiber-entropy defect as a Bregman divergence.}
The defect $D(f,p) = L_p(f) - H_p(x \mid fx)$ should equal
$\sum_b P(b)\,\KL\big(q_b \Vert \unif_{f^{-1}(b)}\big)$ with $P = f_*p$, upgrading
Theorem~\ref{thm:law} from ``the gap is positive off the uniform locus'' to ``the gap is a
distance to that locus'', with joint convexity in $p$ and a Pinsker-type stability bound
$D(f,p) \ge \frac{1}{2\ln 2}\sum_b P(b)\|q_b - \unif_{f^{-1}(b)}\|_1^2$.

\paragraph{B. Bennett tradeoffs for verification.}
Reversible verification that retains a transcript avoids erasure but forces a pebbling
tradeoff between retained checkpoints and recomputation. The conjecture is a family of finite
proof systems for which reversible verification in subexponential auxiliary space necessarily
incurs superlinear recomputation time, while irreversible linear-time verification destroys
linearly many transcript bits.

\paragraph{C. Incompressible proof families.}
For infinitely many lengths $n$, some valid proof object of length $n$ should have
prefix-free description complexity at least $n - O(1)$, and every universal verifier
reconstructing such a proof from a shorter certificate must acquire the missing information
either through its input transcript or through irreversible state changes.

\paragraph{D. Beyond permutative conversions.}
A natural next target is a calculus in which exponential fibers arise from genuine cut
elimination, where normalization can grow proofs, so that normalization time is no longer
linear and the interplay between reduction length and fiber size becomes non-trivial.

\paragraph{E. Multi-stage pipelines.}
Quantifying the worst-case ratio of the $\ell^1$ aggregate to the weighted geometric mean over
$k$-stage pipelines, and identifying the laws that maximize it, would give a sharp measure of
how badly multiplicity accounting can mislead.

\section{Conclusion}

Normalization is forgetting, and forgetting has a price. The Fiber-Entropy Law fixes that
price: counting normalization preimages is thermodynamically exact precisely on
fiberwise-uniform laws and strictly over-charges otherwise. The bureaucratic calculus shows
that the regime where this matters is not exotic --- $n$-bit theorems with $n$-bit canonical
proofs and $n$-step normalization can carry $2^n$ derivations at an exact cost of $n$ bits, or
$n k_B T \ln 2$ of heat. Compositional accounting shows that sharing a lemma saves exactly the
mutual information that verification destroys, never negative but generally less than the
correlation present. And the pipeline dichotomy shows that of the two candidate accountings,
only the entropic one survives composition.

